module bacterialRecoveryQC
module rRNASpeciesDetection
module mRNASusceptibilityDetection

manifest WholeBlood
manifest PBS_BSA_Buffer
manifest SheathFluid
manifest EcoliClinicalIsolate
manifest EcoliClinicalIsolate_Susceptible
manifest EcoliClinicalIsolate_Resistant
manifest RLT_Lysis_Buffer
manifest Ciprofloxacin_Stock

// ============================================================
// Direct Detection and Drug-Resistance Profiling of
// Bacteremias Using Inertial Microfluidics
// Hou, Bhattacharyya, Hung, Han - Lab Chip 2015, 15, 2297
// DOI: 10.1039/c5lc00311c
//
// PROTOCOL OVERVIEW:
//   Two sequential diagnostic workflows are encoded:
//
//   WORKFLOW 1 (Species Identification via rRNA):
//     Bacteria (~100 cfu/mL) are isolated from 3x-diluted
//     whole blood using a PDMS spiral microchannel device
//     (500 um x 80 um, ~10 cm length) operating on Dean
//     Flow Fractionation (DFF). Blood is pumped at 1700 uL/min
//     via syringe pump; sheath fluid (PBS+BSA) at ~10x ratio
//     pins sample at the outer wall. Larger blood cells
//     (RBCs ~6-8 um; leukocytes ~7-12 um) focus inertially
//     at the inner wall and exit the waste outlet. Bacteria
//     (<1 um) and platelets (~2-3 um) follow Dean vortices,
//     complete a full Dean cycle, and exit at the bacterial
//     outlet. Bacterial recovery >65% across 10^2-10^4 cfu/mL.
//     Bacterial eluate is pelleted (16,000 x g), resuspended
//     in 50 uL PBS, lysed with 4x RLT buffer + BME, and
//     analysed by NanoString rRNA hybridization (16S/23S
//     species-specific probes) for pathogen ID without
//     culture or enzymatic amplification. Total time: ~8 h.
//
//   WORKFLOW 2 (Antibiotic Susceptibility via mRNA):
//     Clinical E. coli isolates at ~10^5 cfu/mL (either
//     spiked directly or grown 7.5 h in BACTEC blood culture
//     bottles from 1 cfu/mL) are processed through the same
//     DFF spiral device. Recovered bacteria are exposed to
//     ciprofloxacin (2.5 mcg/mL, 37 C, 30 min), then lysed
//     and analysed by NanoString mRNA detection. A set of 6
//     ciprofloxacin-responsive E. coli transcripts and 3
//     control (non-responsive) transcripts are quantified.
//     The Transcriptional Susceptibility Score (analogous to
//     Squared Pyramidal Distance) discriminates susceptible
//     (score ~0) from resistant (score ~1) strains.
//     Total time: <8 h including separation and RNA detection.
//
// KEY PHYSICAL PARAMETERS:
//   Channel: 500 um (w) x 80 um (h), spiral, ~10 cm length
//   Flow rate: 1700 uL/min total (sample:sheath = 1:10)
//   Sample: 3x diluted whole blood (~15% hematocrit)
//   Throughput: ~20 min per mL of whole blood per chip
//   Bacterial recovery: >65% (vs ~30% by low-speed centrifuge)
//   rRNA detection sensitivity: ~100 cfu/mL (3 mL input)
//   mRNA detection sensitivity: ~10^5 cfu/mL
//
// TEMPERATURE CONTROL:
//   Lysis: room temperature (25 C) for 5 min
//   Ciprofloxacin exposure: 37 C for 30 min
//   NanoString hybridization: 2 h (shortened from standard)
//   `heat` statements model these incubation steps.
//
// NOTE ON SEPARATION ABSTRACTION:
//   The `mix` steps used for DFF separation abstract a
//   continuous-flow spiral microchannel operation, not a DMF
//   electrode mixing event. At each `mix`, the sample stream
//   traverses the spiral under inertial and Dean drag forces.
//   Blood cells exit at the waste outlet (inner wall focus)
//   while bacteria exit at the bacterial outlet (Dean cycle
//   recirculation to outer wall). This is a flow-through
//   fractionation event modelled within BioScript's `mix`
//   abstraction for label-free cell separation.
//
// NOTE ON BIOSCRIPT COMPILER (lilott8/BioScript):
//   - Duration tokens: s and m only (no h). All incubations
//     stated in hours in the paper are converted to minutes.
//   - No array indexing; all droplets are scalar variables.
//   - `split` avoided to prevent stranded droplet compiler
//     errors; dilutions use explicit multi-dispense + mix.
//   - `drain` used for QC fail paths; `dispose` for pass paths.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main                -- entry: sample prep and DFF separation
//   BB2  recoveryPass        -- recoverySignal > 0: adequate bacterial
//                               yield; carry forward to lysis
//   BB3  recoveryFail        -- recoverySignal <= 0: insufficient yield;
//                               drain and abort
//   BB4  recoveryJoin        -- join: rRNA species ID branch begins
//   BB5  rRNAPass            -- rRNASignal > 0: pathogen identified;
//                               dispose and proceed to susceptibility
//   BB6  rRNAFail            -- rRNASignal <= 0: unidentifiable pattern;
//                               drain
//   BB7  rRNAJoin            -- join: mRNA susceptibility branch begins
//   BB8  susceptibilityPass  -- susceptibilitySignal <= 0 (score near 0):
//                               susceptible organism confirmed; dispose
//   BB9  susceptibilityFail  -- susceptibilitySignal > 0 (score near 1):
//                               resistant organism flagged; drain
//   BB10 finalCleanup        -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Prepare 3x-diluted whole blood sample.
//   Fresh human whole blood with EDTA anticoagulant is diluted
//   1:3 (v/v) with PBS + 0.1% BSA (sample buffer). BSA prevents
//   non-specific adsorption to tubing and microchannel walls.
//   Hematocrit target: ~15% (optimal for spiral device).
//   For rRNA experiments: bacteria spiked at ~100 cfu/mL.
//   For mRNA experiments: bacteria spiked at ~10^5 cfu/mL.
//
//   NOTE: Both susceptible and resistant E. coli clinical
//   isolates are processed identically up to ciprofloxacin
//   exposure. A single representative E. coli isolate is
//   encoded here. The susceptible/resistant classification
//   is resolved downstream by the Transcriptional
//   Susceptibility Score in WORKFLOW 2.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

wholeBlood       = dispense 1 units of WholeBlood
sampleBuffer     = dispense 1 units of PBS_BSA_Buffer
bacterialSample  = dispense 1 units of EcoliClinicalIsolate

// 1:3 dilution: mix blood with 3 volumes sample buffer.
// Three buffer units chained to accumulate correct dilution.
buf1             = dispense 1 units of PBS_BSA_Buffer
buf2             = dispense 1 units of PBS_BSA_Buffer

bufferAcc        = mix buf1 with buf2 for 5s
dilutedBlood     = mix wholeBlood with bufferAcc for 5s

// Spike bacteria into diluted blood immediately before loading.
spikedBloodSample = mix dilutedBlood with bacterialSample for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Prime the spiral microchannel device.
//   Load 1x PBS + 0.1% BSA as sheath fluid via inner inlet
//   (425 um width) at ~10x the sample flow rate to pin the
//   sample stream near the outer wall at the inlet.
//   Device is pre-wetted to remove air bubbles and
//   condition channel walls to minimise non-specific adhesion.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sheathPrime = dispense 1 units of SheathFluid
devicePrime = mix sheathPrime with spikedBloodSample for 2s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: DFF separation - inertial focusing of blood cells
//   and Dean migration of bacteria.
//   Spiral device operated at 1700 uL/min total flow.
//   Sample loaded at outer inlet (75 um width); sheath at
//   inner inlet (425 um width). Sample:sheath = 1:10.
//   Over ~10 cm spiral length, RBCs and leukocytes experience
//   dominant inertial lift (F_L) and focus at inner wall.
//   Bacteria (<1 um, ap/h << 0.07) are unaffected by F_L and
//   follow Dean drag (F_D), completing a full Dean Cycle (DC1)
//   to recirculate to the outer wall and exit the bacterial
//   outlet (200 um width).
//   Process time: ~20 min per mL whole blood.
//
//   `mix` abstracts this continuous-flow separation event.
//   Output `bacterialEluate` represents the fluid collected
//   from the bacterial outlet; `bloodWaste` represents the
//   inner-wall (waste) outlet stream.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sheathFlow     = dispense 1 units of SheathFluid
bacterialEluate = mix devicePrime with sheathFlow for 20m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Concentrate bacterial eluate.
//   Eluate collected from bacterial outlet is pelleted by
//   centrifugation at 16,000 x g. Supernatant removed.
//   Pellet resuspended in 50 uL 1x PBS for downstream steps.
//   `heat` at 25 C (room temperature) abstracts centrifugation
//   and resuspension as a defined physical processing step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat bacterialEluate at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: QC - verify adequate bacterial recovery.
//   In the paper, recovery is verified by cfu counting on
//   LB agar plates (>65% recovery expected across tested
//   concentrations). This QC gate ensures sufficient bacterial
//   material for downstream RNA detection before proceeding.
//   bacterialRecoveryQC module abstracts the cfu enumeration.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

recoverySignal = detect bacterialRecoveryQC on bacterialEluate for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Recovery QC gate
// Pass (BB2): recoverySignal > 0 -> adequate bacterial yield
//             (>65% recovery); carry forward to lysis.
// Fail (BB3): recoverySignal <= 0 -> insufficient bacteria
//             recovered; drain and abort.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (recoveryJoin).
// ============================================================

if (recoverySignal > 0) {
    dispose bacterialEluate
} else {
    drain bacterialEluate
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: recoveryJoin
// Dispose recovery QC signal. Control falls into rRNA
// species identification branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose recoverySignal

// ============================================================
// WORKFLOW 1: Species Identification via rRNA Detection
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Bacterial lysis for RNA extraction.
//   Concentrated pellet (50 uL in PBS) is lysed by vigorous
//   vortexing with 4x excess of RLT buffer (Qiagen) containing
//   beta-mercaptoethanol. Incubated 5 min at room temperature.
//   Lysate used immediately or stored at -80 C.
//   `heat` at 25 C models RT lysis incubation.
//   RLT buffer denatures proteins and inactivates RNases to
//   preserve RNA integrity for NanoString hybridization.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bacterialPellet = dispense 1 units of WholeBlood

// Model: re-dispense of concentrated pellet as starting input.
// In practice this is the resuspended pellet from STEP 4.
rltBuffer        = dispense 1 units of RLT_Lysis_Buffer
bacterialLysate  = mix bacterialPellet with rltBuffer for 5m
heat bacterialLysate at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: NanoString rRNA hybridization for species ID.
//   Crude lysate input to NanoString assay. Two adjacent
//   50-nt probes hybridise to species-variable regions of
//   16S and 23S rRNA (>1000-fold more abundant than mRNA).
//   Probeset: 13 discriminatory probes targeting E. coli (Ec),
//   K. pneumoniae (Kp), P. aeruginosa (PsA), and Ec/Kp shared.
//   Hybridization time: 2 h (shortened from standard protocol
//   with minimal signal loss). PrepStation immobilises probes;
//   nCounter Digital Analyzer reads fluorescent barcodes.
//   Normalized Organism Score = geometric mean of
//   species-specific probe counts / sum of all geometric means.
//   Samples at 100 cfu/mL processed from 3 mL whole blood.
//   Hybridization modelled as `heat` at 65 C for 120 min.
//
//   NOTE: Direct detection from whole blood (without DFF)
//   produces uninterpretable hybridization patterns due to
//   interference from blood products. DFF pre-processing is
//   required for valid rRNA signal.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat bacterialLysate at 65c for 120m
rRNASignal = detect rRNASpeciesDetection on bacterialLysate for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: rRNA species identification QC gate
// Pass (BB5): rRNASignal > 0 -> Organism Score assigns
//             species identity unambiguously; dispose lysate
//             and proceed to susceptibility workflow.
// Fail (BB6): rRNASignal <= 0 -> uninterpretable pattern
//             (e.g. insufficient bacterial load or mixed
//             infection outside probeset); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (rRNAJoin).
// ============================================================

if (rRNASignal > 0) {
    dispose bacterialLysate
} else {
    drain bacterialLysate
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: rRNAJoin
// Dispose rRNA QC signal. Control falls into mRNA
// antibiotic susceptibility branch (BB8/BB9).
// ============================================================

dispose rRNASignal

// ============================================================
// WORKFLOW 2: Antibiotic Susceptibility via mRNA Detection
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Prepare bacteria at mRNA-detectable load.
//   Two input routes (both encoded here in sequence to
//   represent both experimental conditions in Fig. 5):
//
//   Route A (Direct spike): E. coli spiked at 10^5 cfu/mL
//     into 1 mL whole blood. Processed immediately by DFF.
//
//   Route B (Blood culture): BACTEC blood culture bottle
//     inoculated at 1 cfu/mL of E. coli; incubated 7.5 h at
//     37 C to reach >10^5 cfu/mL. Cultured blood then
//     processed by DFF (1:5 v/v dilution with culture media).
//     7.5 h = 450 min. `heat` models BACTEC incubation.
//
//   NOTE: NanoString mRNA detection requires ~10^5 cfu/mL,
//   which is >1 order of magnitude better than the VITEK-2
//   threshold (>10^6 cfu/mL) but exceeds typical bacteremia
//   loads (~10-100 cfu/mL). Blood culture pre-enrichment
//   bridges this gap while still being faster than current
//   gold-standard susceptibility methods.
//
//   Both susceptible and resistant isolates are encoded as
//   separate manifests; the Transcriptional Susceptibility
//   Score resolves their classification downstream.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Route A: Direct spike at 10^5 cfu/mL
ecoli_S          = dispense 1 units of EcoliClinicalIsolate_Susceptible
ecoli_R          = dispense 1 units of EcoliClinicalIsolate_Resistant

bloodForS        = dispense 1 units of WholeBlood
bloodForR        = dispense 1 units of WholeBlood

bufS1            = dispense 1 units of PBS_BSA_Buffer
bufS2            = dispense 1 units of PBS_BSA_Buffer
bufAccS          = mix bufS1 with bufS2 for 5s
dilutedBloodS    = mix bloodForS with bufAccS for 5s
spikedBloodS     = mix dilutedBloodS with ecoli_S for 5s

bufR1            = dispense 1 units of PBS_BSA_Buffer
bufR2            = dispense 1 units of PBS_BSA_Buffer
bufAccR          = mix bufR1 with bufR2 for 5s
dilutedBloodR    = mix bloodForR with bufAccR for 5s
spikedBloodR     = mix dilutedBloodR with ecoli_R for 5s

// Route B: Blood culture pre-enrichment (7.5 h at 37 C = 450 min)
// Modelled as heat step on the susceptible isolate sample.
// In practice, BACTEC bottle incubated off-chip before DFF.
heat spikedBloodS at 37c for 450m
heat spikedBloodR at 37c for 450m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: DFF separation of bacteria from cultured blood.
//   Cultured blood (or directly spiked blood) processed
//   through the same spiral device at 1700 uL/min.
//   Bacteria at 10^5 cfu/mL are recovered at the bacterial
//   outlet with >65% efficiency. `mix` abstracts DFF event.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sheathS          = dispense 1 units of SheathFluid
sheathR          = dispense 1 units of SheathFluid

eluateS          = mix spikedBloodS with sheathS for 20m
eluateR          = mix spikedBloodR with sheathR for 20m

// Concentrate bacterial eluates at RT (room temperature).
heat eluateS at 25c for 5m
heat eluateR at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Ciprofloxacin exposure.
//   Concentrated bacterial fractions are exposed to
//   ciprofloxacin at 2.5 mcg/mL for 30 min at 37 C.
//   Ciprofloxacin (fluoroquinolone) inhibits DNA gyrase.
//   In susceptible organisms: transcriptional stress response
//   upregulates 6 ciprofloxacin-responsive mRNA transcripts.
//   In resistant organisms: these transcripts are not induced
//   at the same concentration. This differential expression
//   forms the basis of the Transcriptional Susceptibility Score.
//   30 min exposure is sufficient to generate a detectable
//   transcriptional signal without extensive bacterial growth.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cipro_S          = dispense 1 units of Ciprofloxacin_Stock
cipro_R          = dispense 1 units of Ciprofloxacin_Stock

ciproExposure_S  = mix eluateS with cipro_S for 5s
ciproExposure_R  = mix eluateR with cipro_R for 5s

heat ciproExposure_S at 37c for 30m
heat ciproExposure_R at 37c for 30m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: Lysis and NanoString mRNA detection.
//   Ciprofloxacin-exposed bacteria are lysed with RLT +
//   beta-mercaptoethanol (4x excess, 5 min RT).
//   NanoString probeset: 6 ciprofloxacin-responsive E. coli
//   mRNA transcripts + 3 control (non-responsive) transcripts.
//   Responsive transcripts normalised to the geometric mean
//   of control transcript expression to control for cell
//   number variation. Transcriptional Susceptibility Score
//   projected onto vector between centroid of susceptible
//   (score ~0) and resistant (score ~1) strains.
//   Hybridization: 2 h at 65 C.
//
//   NOTE: score <= 0 threshold (dashed line = 4 SD from
//   susceptible derivation cohort mean) identifies susceptible
//   isolates; score > 0 identifies resistant isolates.
//   mRNASusceptibilityDetection module abstracts this scoring.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rltLysis_S       = dispense 1 units of RLT_Lysis_Buffer
rltLysis_R       = dispense 1 units of RLT_Lysis_Buffer

lysate_S         = mix ciproExposure_S with rltLysis_S for 5m
lysate_R         = mix ciproExposure_R with rltLysis_R for 5m

heat lysate_S at 25c for 5m
heat lysate_R at 25c for 5m

// NanoString mRNA hybridization: 2 h at 65 C
heat lysate_S at 65c for 120m
heat lysate_R at 65c for 120m

// Detect Transcriptional Susceptibility Score for each isolate.
// Score near 0: susceptible-like. Score near 1: resistant-like.
susceptibilitySignal_S = detect mRNASusceptibilityDetection on lysate_S for 30s
susceptibilitySignal_R = detect mRNASusceptibilityDetection on lysate_R for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Susceptibility classification gate
// The gate is keyed on susceptibilitySignal_S (susceptible
// isolate), which should score near 0 (signal <= 0 in the
// BioScript boolean sense = susceptible confirmed).
//
// Pass (BB8): susceptibilitySignal_S <= 0 -> score near 0,
//             susceptible-like transcriptional profile;
//             dispose both lysates.
// Fail (BB9): susceptibilitySignal_S > 0 -> score near 1,
//             resistant-like or ambiguous profile; drain both.
//
// NOTE: In the paper, both susceptible and resistant strains
// are clearly separated by the score (Fig. 5B). Here the
// gate logic reflects that a susceptible sample correctly
// classified has susceptibilitySignal_S <= 0. A resistant
// sample processed as lysate_S would fail this gate (> 0),
// correctly flagging resistance.
// ============================================================

if (susceptibilitySignal_S <= 0) {
    dispose lysate_S
    dispose lysate_R
} else {
    drain lysate_S
    drain lysate_R
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all susceptibility QC signal droplets.
// Experiment ends. Full workflow time: ~8 h including
// DFF separation (~20 min/mL), RNA hybridization (~2 h),
// and ciprofloxacin exposure (30 min).
// ============================================================

dispose susceptibilitySignal_S
dispose susceptibilitySignal_R

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
